<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Wagons South and Wagons West on Berrytree.org</title><link>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/</link><description>Recent content in Wagons South and Wagons West on Berrytree.org</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><atom:link href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Chapter I: Origin of the Surname</title><link>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch01-origin-of-the-surname/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch01-origin-of-the-surname/</guid><description>&lt;div class="ancestor-page"&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;header class="book-banner" role="banner"&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/"&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="book-banner-title"&gt;Wagons South and Wagons West&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="book-banner-author"&gt;by Glenn Berry&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/header&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;div class="book-chapter-nav"&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/bibliography/"&gt;← Bibliography&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/"&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&lt;hr&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="chapter-i"&gt;Chapter I&lt;a class="anchor" href="#chapter-i"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;h1 id="origin-of-the-surname"&gt;Origin of the Surname&lt;a class="anchor" href="#origin-of-the-surname"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;The family name BERRY is a good example of the evolutionary nature of names. People took as surnames the names of flowers, fish, animals, birds, and occupations. But by far, one of the most common sources of surnames was place names. The name BERRY is a derivative of the Old English suffix &lt;em&gt;burg&lt;/em&gt;, indicating one who lived at or near a hill, stronghold, or fortified place. Thus, the surname BERRY became a frequent name in a large category of surnames derived from place names. Because of the evolutionary nature of name development, it is virtually impossible to pinpoint the exact date of the formation of any new name. BERRY, of course, is no exception. However, some of the ancient records of the English nation give evidence of early forms of the name and show usage as early as 1513. (&lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/bibliography/#ref-III"&gt;III; p.3&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Chapter II: Family Crest &amp; Coat of Arms</title><link>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch02-family-crest-and-coat-of-arms/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch02-family-crest-and-coat-of-arms/</guid><description>&lt;div class="ancestor-page"&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;header class="book-banner" role="banner"&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/"&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="book-banner-title"&gt;Wagons South and Wagons West&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="book-banner-author"&gt;by Glenn Berry&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/header&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;div class="book-chapter-nav"&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch01-origin-of-the-surname/"&gt;← Chapter I: Origin of the Surname&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/"&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch03-immigration-to-america/"&gt;Chapter III: Immigration to America →&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&lt;hr&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="chapter-ii"&gt;Chapter II&lt;a class="anchor" href="#chapter-ii"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;h1 id="family-crest--coat-of-arms"&gt;Family Crest &amp;amp; Coat-of-Arms&lt;a class="anchor" href="#family-crest--coat-of-arms"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;h3 id="berry"&gt;BERRY&lt;a class="anchor" href="#berry"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;&#10;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;ARMS: Vert a cross crosslet or. CREST: From a coronet (set with crosses) a goat&amp;rsquo;s head proper. ARMS: On a green field, a gold cross with the four ends of the cross crossed. CREST: From a coronet [set with crosses] a goat&amp;rsquo;s head in its natural color.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Chapter III: Immigration to America</title><link>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch03-immigration-to-america/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch03-immigration-to-america/</guid><description>&lt;div class="ancestor-page"&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;header class="book-banner" role="banner"&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/"&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="book-banner-title"&gt;Wagons South and Wagons West&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="book-banner-author"&gt;by Glenn Berry&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/header&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;div class="book-chapter-nav"&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch02-family-crest-and-coat-of-arms/"&gt;← Chapter II: Family Crest &amp;amp; Coat of Arms&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/"&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch04-the-revolutionary-war/"&gt;Chapter IV: The Revolutionary War →&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&lt;hr&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="chapter-iii"&gt;Chapter III&lt;a class="anchor" href="#chapter-iii"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;h1 id="ships-ahoy-immigration-to-america"&gt;Ships Ahoy (Immigration to America)&lt;a class="anchor" href="#ships-ahoy-immigration-to-america"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&amp;hellip;Every American is either an immigrant or has descended from immigrants, except possibly the American Indians. The story of American immigration is the story&amp;hellip;of an estimated seventy (70) million people of different backgrounds &amp;amp; nationalities. It is the story of their hopes, their fears, their aspirations: it is the story of America itself&amp;hellip;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Chapter IV: The Revolutionary War</title><link>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch04-the-revolutionary-war/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch04-the-revolutionary-war/</guid><description>&lt;div class="ancestor-page"&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;header class="book-banner" role="banner"&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/"&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="book-banner-title"&gt;Wagons South and Wagons West&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="book-banner-author"&gt;by Glenn Berry&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/header&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;div class="book-chapter-nav"&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch03-immigration-to-america/"&gt;← Chapter III: Immigration to America&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/"&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch05-wagons-south/"&gt;Chapter V: Wagons South →&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&lt;hr&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="chapter-iv"&gt;Chapter IV&lt;a class="anchor" href="#chapter-iv"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;h1 id="the-british-are-coming-the-revolutionary-war"&gt;The British Are Coming (The Revolutionary War)&lt;a class="anchor" href="#the-british-are-coming-the-revolutionary-war"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;As I begin writing this chapter, it is not known the name of the BERRY ancestor, if any, who fought to gain Independence for America from England.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Chapter V: Wagons South</title><link>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch05-wagons-south/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch05-wagons-south/</guid><description>&lt;div class="ancestor-page"&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;header class="book-banner" role="banner"&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/"&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="book-banner-title"&gt;Wagons South and Wagons West&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="book-banner-author"&gt;by Glenn Berry&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/header&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;div class="book-chapter-nav"&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch04-the-revolutionary-war/"&gt;← Chapter IV: The Revolutionary War&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/"&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch06-away-down-south-in-dixie/"&gt;Chapter VI: Away Down South in Dixie →&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&lt;hr&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="chapter-v"&gt;Chapter V&lt;a class="anchor" href="#chapter-v"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;h1 id="wagons-south--alabama--heart-of-dixie"&gt;Wagons South — Alabama — &amp;ldquo;Heart of Dixie&amp;rdquo;&lt;a class="anchor" href="#wagons-south--alabama--heart-of-dixie"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;h3 id="a-state-of-alabama-created-december-14-1819"&gt;A. State of Alabama created December 14, 1819&lt;a class="anchor" href="#a-state-of-alabama-created-december-14-1819"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Some native-born Americans from the 13 Original Colonies trickled into Alabama while the British ruled here. After the Revolutionary War ended, the trickle became an increasing stream&amp;hellip;A boundary dispute with Spain involving Alabama was settled by the Treaty of San Lorenzo 1795. (&lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/bibliography/#ref-I"&gt;I; p.123&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Chapter VI: Away Down South in Dixie</title><link>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch06-away-down-south-in-dixie/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch06-away-down-south-in-dixie/</guid><description>&lt;div class="ancestor-page"&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;header class="book-banner" role="banner"&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/"&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="book-banner-title"&gt;Wagons South and Wagons West&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="book-banner-author"&gt;by Glenn Berry&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/header&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;div class="book-chapter-nav"&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch05-wagons-south/"&gt;← Chapter V: Wagons South&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/"&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch07-in-the-land-of-cotton/"&gt;Chapter VII: In the Land of Cotton →&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&lt;hr&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="chapter-vi"&gt;Chapter VI&lt;a class="anchor" href="#chapter-vi"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;h1 id="away-down-south-in-dixie-1820-1830"&gt;&amp;hellip;Away Down South in Dixie&amp;hellip; (1820-1830)&lt;a class="anchor" href="#away-down-south-in-dixie-1820-1830"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;A group of BERRY pioneers settled in Blount County Alabama at Brooksville in about 1815. Kenneth E. Lee, now deceased, published a history on this &amp;ldquo;set&amp;rdquo; of BERRY&amp;rsquo;S in 1981. In 1985, his brother Arthur C. Lee, published an additional book completed and up-dated on this &amp;ldquo;set&amp;rdquo; of BERRY&amp;rsquo;S.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Chapter VII: In the Land of Cotton</title><link>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch07-in-the-land-of-cotton/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch07-in-the-land-of-cotton/</guid><description>&lt;div class="ancestor-page"&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;header class="book-banner" role="banner"&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/"&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="book-banner-title"&gt;Wagons South and Wagons West&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="book-banner-author"&gt;by Glenn Berry&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/header&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;div class="book-chapter-nav"&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch06-away-down-south-in-dixie/"&gt;← Chapter VI: Away Down South in Dixie&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/"&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch08-i-wish-i-was-in-dixie/"&gt;Chapter VIII: I Wish I Was in Dixie →&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&lt;hr&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="chapter-vii"&gt;Chapter VII&lt;a class="anchor" href="#chapter-vii"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;h1 id="in-the-land-of-cotton"&gt;&amp;hellip;In the Land of Cotton&amp;hellip;&lt;a class="anchor" href="#in-the-land-of-cotton"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Clothmaking in the North demanded the use of much cotton, and cotton came from the South. Before 1800, tobacco had been the all-important crop of the South, but after 1800, cotton began to take over that role. This was due to the increased need for cotton in Northern manufacturing centers and to the invention of a machine to pick seeds from cotton, called the Cotton Gin. (&lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/bibliography/#ref-XIII"&gt;XIII; pp.274-275&lt;/a&gt;) In 1793, Eli Whitney invented the Cotton Gin (engine). This machine is described below:&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Chapter VIII: I Wish I Was in Dixie</title><link>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch08-i-wish-i-was-in-dixie/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch08-i-wish-i-was-in-dixie/</guid><description>&lt;div class="ancestor-page"&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;header class="book-banner" role="banner"&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/"&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="book-banner-title"&gt;Wagons South and Wagons West&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="book-banner-author"&gt;by Glenn Berry&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/header&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;div class="book-chapter-nav"&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch07-in-the-land-of-cotton/"&gt;← Chapter VII: In the Land of Cotton&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/"&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch09-texas-fever-in-the-air/"&gt;Chapter IX: Texas Fever in the Air →&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&lt;hr&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="chapter-viii"&gt;Chapter VIII&lt;a class="anchor" href="#chapter-viii"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;h1 id="i-wish-i-was-in-dixie-1830-1840"&gt;&amp;hellip;I Wish I Was in Dixie&amp;hellip; (1830-1840)&lt;a class="anchor" href="#i-wish-i-was-in-dixie-1830-1840"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;The first Federal Census of Fayette County Alabama is for the year 1830. The following BERRY men are listed as Head of Household:&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Chapter IX: Texas Fever in the Air</title><link>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch09-texas-fever-in-the-air/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch09-texas-fever-in-the-air/</guid><description>&lt;div class="ancestor-page"&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;header class="book-banner" role="banner"&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/"&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="book-banner-title"&gt;Wagons South and Wagons West&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="book-banner-author"&gt;by Glenn Berry&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/header&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;div class="book-chapter-nav"&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch08-i-wish-i-was-in-dixie/"&gt;← Chapter VIII: I Wish I Was in Dixie&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/"&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch10-in-dixie-land-where-i-was-born/"&gt;Chapter X: In Dixie Land Where I Was Born →&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&lt;hr&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="chapter-ix"&gt;Chapter IX&lt;a class="anchor" href="#chapter-ix"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;h1 id="texas-fever-in-the-air"&gt;Texas Fever in the Air&lt;a class="anchor" href="#texas-fever-in-the-air"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;During the 1830&amp;rsquo;s and from thence onward, &amp;ldquo;Texas Fever&amp;rdquo; caught hold of many Alabamians. Many pioneers left the State of Alabama and County of Fayette before they had very deep roots established there.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Chapter X: In Dixie Land Where I Was Born</title><link>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch10-in-dixie-land-where-i-was-born/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch10-in-dixie-land-where-i-was-born/</guid><description>&lt;div class="ancestor-page"&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;header class="book-banner" role="banner"&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/"&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="book-banner-title"&gt;Wagons South and Wagons West&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="book-banner-author"&gt;by Glenn Berry&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/header&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;div class="book-chapter-nav"&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch09-texas-fever-in-the-air/"&gt;← Chapter IX: Texas Fever in the Air&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/"&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&lt;hr&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="chapter-x"&gt;Chapter X&lt;a class="anchor" href="#chapter-x"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;h1 id="in-dixie-land-where-i-was-born-1840-1850"&gt;&amp;hellip;In Dixie Land Where I Was Born&amp;hellip; (1840-1850)&lt;a class="anchor" href="#in-dixie-land-where-i-was-born-1840-1850"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;The Federal Census of Fayette County Alabama 1840 lists the following BERRY pioneer men as Head of Household:&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Chapter XI: Look Away Dixie Land</title><link>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch11-look-away-dixie-land/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch11-look-away-dixie-land/</guid><description>&lt;div class="ancestor-page"&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;header class="book-banner" role="banner"&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/"&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="book-banner-title"&gt;Wagons South and Wagons West&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="book-banner-author"&gt;by Glenn Berry&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/header&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;div class="book-chapter-nav"&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/ch10-in-dixie-land-where-i-was-born/"&gt;← Chapter X: In Dixie Land Where I Was Born&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href="https://berrytree.org/books/wagons-south-wagons-west/"&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&lt;hr&gt;&#10;&l